  "armory.guide.naturalLight": "Shoot in natural light",
  "armory.guide.background": "Keep the background clean",
  "armory.guide.cleanGear.body": "Do not submit photos of rusty armor — polish it up before shooting. Rust and neglect are red flags for Authenticity Officers.",
  "armory.guide.modernGear.body": "Wear historically accurate medieval shoes or boots. Never wear sneakers or modern trainers in your submission photos.",
  "armory.guide.twoSets.body": "Take one set fully armored but without your tabard/surcoat so the Authenticity Officers can see the actual steel. Take a second set wearing your tabard over your armor to show your team heraldry.",
  "armory.guide.fullBody.body": "Capture Front, Back, and both Sides. Stand straight with your arms slightly out to the sides so nothing is hidden.",
  "armory.guide.helmet.body": "Show all sides of the helmet. Include a close-up of the inside to verify the suspension and padding, and a close-up of the visor and breathing holes.",
  "armory.guide.armsLegs.body": "Close-up shots of gauntlets, pauldrons (shoulders), couters (elbows), poleyns (knees), greaves, and sabatons. No coverage gaps.",
  "armory.guide.torso.body": "A focused shot of your cuirass, brigandine, or coat-of-plates showing the full front and back coverage.",
  "armory.guide.sourceDocs.body": "Have your source documents ready before submitting. If the Authenticity Committee requests it, provide the manuscript illustration, effigy photograph, or museum reference your specific armor piece is based on.",
  "armory.guide.manuscripts.body": "Period manuscripts (e.g. Codex Manesse, Maciejowski Bible), tomb effigies, and museum reconstructions are all valid references. Note the date and collection so officers can verify easily.",
  "armory.guide.keepCopies.body": "Save digital scans or photos of your references in this set alongside your gear photos. That way everything is in one place when the committee reviews your submission.",
  "armory.guide.naturalLight.body": "Take photos outdoors on an overcast day, or stand directly facing a large window. Harsh noon sunlight creates deep shadows that hide details; dim indoor light blurs the metal.",
  "armory.guide.background.body": "Shoot against a plain wall — brick, garage door, or plain plaster all work well. Avoid cluttered gyms, cars, or messy rooms so officers can focus strictly on the gear.",
